+# [MP8] Tint the device visual on the player's back to c (Color.WHITE = restore the original color).
+#
+# Why this is needed at all — **the device you see on your back isn't the device itself**. The moment the device attaches it
+# sets `visible = false` and gets moved 100 m underground (attach_entered_rpc). The one on your back is the player scene's
+# own `spine_breaker_visual`, a separate set of meshes — so the red tint applied to the device itself
+# "disappears" as soon as it grabs someone (user tested in practice on 2026-08-17). This tints the set on the back separately.
+#
+# It also uses set_surface_override_material (node level, one copy per player)
+# and doesn't touch the material resource itself — see the _mp8_tint_device section in the minigame script for why.
+# ⚠️⚠️ This function was wrong in **two versions** in a row, so the lessons are written down here for good.
+#
+#   The first version used `spine_breaker_visual.find_children(...)` — it didn't tint a single surface.
+#     `find_children()` **only searches descendants, not the node itself**, and `spine_breaker_visual` is itself a
+#     MeshInstance3D. On top of that its surface 0 material is null, which was also filtered out by "must be StandardMaterial3D".
+#
+#   The second version fixed both of those, and the log faithfully printed `tinted 1 surface` — **but nothing changed on screen**.
+#     It tinted the right node but the wrong object. `spine_breaker_visual` (SpineBreakerAttached)
+#     isn't the device shown on screen at all. The device actually visible on the player's back is
+#     the bone that `set_spinebreaker_visuals()` scales from 0 to 1 with `skeleton_attached.set_bone_pose_scale(34, ...)`
+#     — it belongs to **a different skeleton**
+#     (`Visuals/main character spinebreaker attached`), and is **surface 3** of its character mesh.
+#
+# ⭐⭐ The shared lesson from both — **"I changed it" and "the player can see it" are two different things.**
+#    The log saying it tinted 1 surface was true, it's just that nobody ever sees that surface. Verification has to happen on screen.
+#
+# The current approach (found with probe46, no guessing at indices) is **matching by texture name**.
+# The device surface and the device itself use a texture with the same name, each just a copy under its own model folder.
+#     Device itself    spinebreaker single2**_spinebreaker base1.png**
+#     Player surf 3    character spinebreaker attached1**_spinebreaker base1.png**
+#     Character skin   ..._main character painted6 / hands boots / eye open   ← no match
+# So the part "after the last underscore" is used as the key, scanning the whole Visuals subtree.
+# The upside is that **carrying it around and the execution animation share this mesh**, so one rule covers both.
+#
+# Empty tex_key = no matching (falls back to "tint every surface that has a texture"), which only happens when the texture can't be read from the device.
+func mp8_set_visual_tint(c: Color, tex_key: String = "") -> int :
+
+	# First restore whatever was tinted last time — the previous device may have been red and this one white, so it has to be able to switch back.
+	# It records **the original override material** instead of always clearing to null. Character customization (customization assigner)
+	# may also have put override materials on these surfaces, and clearing them all would wipe the player's colors.
+	for row in _mp8_tinted:
+		var old_mi = row[0]
+		if is_instance_valid(old_mi):
+			old_mi.set_surface_override_material(row[1], row[2])
+	_mp8_tinted.clear()
+
+	if c == Color.WHITE or visuals == null:
+		return 0
+
+	var n: int = 0
+
+	for node in visuals.find_children("", "MeshInstance3D", true, false):
+
+		var mi: MeshInstance3D = node
+		if mi.mesh == null:
+			continue
+
+		for s in mi.mesh.get_surface_count():
+
+			var src = mi.get_active_material(s)
+			if not (src is StandardMaterial3D):
+				continue
+
+			var sd: StandardMaterial3D = src
+			if sd.albedo_texture == null:
+				continue
+
+			if tex_key != "":
+				var f: String = str(sd.albedo_texture.resource_path).get_file()
+				if not f.ends_with(tex_key):
+					continue
+
+			var m: StandardMaterial3D = sd.duplicate()
+			m.albedo_color = c
+			_mp8_tinted.append([mi, s, mi.get_surface_override_material(s)])
+			mi.set_surface_override_material(s, m)
+			n += 1
+
+	return n
+
+
+# [MP8] The device currently on my back. If none is bound, fall back to the vanilla one (with one device they're the same)
+func _mp8_dev() -> SpineBreakerDevice :
+
+	if mp8_current_device != null and is_instance_valid(mp8_current_device):
+		return mp8_current_device
+	return spine_breaker_device
+
